   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3ab95b6d-b8ce-41d2-b846-c60ddccd6665",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Now, the resulting soup-object is a lot easier to read as it is strutured with linebreaks and indents.\n",
    "We can now search/filter for the categories/objects, we are interested in (our desired data).\n",
    "\n",
    "Before doing so, we should have a look at common errors, that could arise during accessing the url."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3d65aec8-0ad2-4a27-9d18-8ee236dd6065",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<h4> 5.2 Defining headers and user agents </h4>\n",
    "\n",
    "Of course, website designers are aware that people might try to scrape information from their page and can try to prevent/restrict this (because it is a lot of requests in a short amount of time: servercapacity). They can, for example, require that pages can only be accessed by a computers web-browser. If we would try to access the webpage from a python script (i.e. through a Jupyter Notebook or Visual Studio Code), the webpage would detect us as a non-bowser and deny the access.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Often, a solution to this problem is to specify a user agent, Python is supposed to use when accessing the webpage.\n",
    "Such a user agent string contains information on the browser being used, the operating system or the different versions and enables a website to provide a suitable format.\n",
    "\n",
    "Specifying and using such a user agent, we can hide that accessing the webpage actually happens out of Python (for scraping-purposes).\n",
    "\n",
    "In Firefox you can use the URL `about:support` to find a table which contains the user-agent. \n",
    "In Google-Chrome type `chrome://version` into the url field, to find the information.\n",
    "Other than that, there are several websites, which read and return you agent, i.e. [here](http://wieistmeinuseragent.de/)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "8fb9e7eb-01bb-47db-b6de-6f38826f0ae2",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Example code which fails due to missing user agent\n",
    "url = 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Konstanz'\n",
    "\n",
    "resp = requests.get(url)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Response status code: \" + str(resp.status_code))\n",
    "\n",
    "# And saving the content in a soup object\n",
    "soup = BeautifulSoup(resp.content, 'html.parser')\n",
    "soup"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "043b8705-fb58-45b3-a665-ee4e4e593f80",
   "metadata": {
    "jupyter": {
     "outputs_hidden": true
    },
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Example code accessing the same page using a user agent\n",
    "# INCLUDE YOU USER AGENT HERE (in quotation marks)\n",
    "# i.e. Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64; rv:131.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/131.0\n",
    "headers = {\"user-agent\": \"Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/130.0.0.0 Safari/537.36\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "# Adding the parameter headers, which we just defined\n",
    "resp = requests.get(url, headers = headers)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Response status code: \" + str(resp.status_code))\n",
    "\n",
    "# And saving the content in a soup object\n",
    "soup = BeautifulSoup(resp.content, 'html.parser')\n",
    "soup"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5d9fb274-64cd-4e8f-af05-48874c8a9a52",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We saw, trying to access the wikipedia website without a specified user agent failed (while the soccer-website beforehand worked even without). For the wikipedia page, we needed to set the user agent, to \"hide\" that we are trying to scrape the information."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "64cfca08-2a2f-45aa-b619-70e23ee91778",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": []
   },
   "source": [
    "<h4> 5.3 Selecting/Filtering for the required data </h4>\n",
    "\n",
    "After sucessfully accessing the website and saving the HTML content into a soup-object, we now have to turn on to filter or select the actual information (the elements), we are interested in from the entire content.\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore, we need the different hierarchy-levels and HTML-object types, which are used to build and structure the website.\n",
    "\n",
    "We will, for a second, turn back to the ICSS website to see a first example of filtering for these HTML elements."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "aa760d88-b288-4185-a802-5ee21184b6a0",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# opening the file (slightly differenet than a usual url)\n",
    "\n",
    "with open('website/index.html', 'r') as f:\n",
    "    html = f.read()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "af0975c5-1933-4e46-9214-200c5a41a8e9",
   "metadata": {
    "jupyter": {
     "outputs_hidden": true
    },
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(html)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5d218cf9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**HTML tree structure**\n",
    "\n",
    "As we can see above, the HTML code has a tree-structure with different hierarchy levels. The root is the html-object. Going down the levels we have the children (the header and body) and within each of them other cildren (i.e. div (text), div (link) and div(image).\n",
    "\n",
    "Usually, we would use Beautiful Soup to parse the code (although the hierarchy levels are easier to recognize in the ``print(html)`` code:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "b8b57ac6-f78e-4b4d-a568-6ce5318bf47c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "soup = BeautifulSoup(html, 'html.parser')\n",
    "soup"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8364ede1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The 'soup' is now our root object and we can access its children using the dot-syntax, i.e. ``soup.body`` or ``soup.body.div``. Note that this syntax returns the first match, here the divider with \"text-container\" but not e.g. the \"image-container\"."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "c40d1b27",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "soup.body.div"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a5175d7d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Using the commands\n",
    "\n",
    "- ``soup.body.children``\n",
    "- ``soup.body.descendants``\n",
    "\n",
    "you can get all the children or descendants of an object in the HTML-tree. Since the two commands return iterators, you will have to use them in some kind of loop, i.e. ``for child in soup.body.children``.\n",
    "\n",
    "You can not only walk downwards the tree, but also upwards (using the ``.parent`` syntax to find the one-hierarchy-level higher element) or sidewards, to find the siblings on the same hierarch-level (using ``.next_sibling`` or ``.previous_sibling``). Again, you will have to iterate over these parents/siblings."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "2a4f75fb",
   "metadata": {
    "scrolled": true
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# getting all the soup's children\n",
    "\n",
    "for child in soup.body.children:\n",
    "    print(child)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "bf717336-ed81-4fc4-a6e2-8ad0c04f9a84",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**soup.find()**\n",
    "\n",
    "If you want to select a specific item you can do so by specifying the **tag** under which the information is stored. Here, we want to get the `title` of the website"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "451729d2-5a4d-4265-abde-11bbc64d7b9a",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "soup.find('title')\n",
    "# or\n",
    "soup.title\n",
    "\n",
    "# soup.title.text for the pure content"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "84ba6304-8a5d-4400-b748-1aed3a71c634",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Additionally, you can **refine the search** for elements by defining their **attributes**. Here, for example, we want the section (`div`-tag) with the class/type 'image-container'. There are several ways to enter such a search:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "4821f10f-d497-4871-a9a3-cefd5387a5f0",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "soup.find('div', class_='image-container')\n",
    "\n",
    "# or\n",
    "soup.find('div', {'class':'image-container'})\n",
    "\n",
    "# or\n",
    "soup.find(attrs={'class':'image-container'})\n",
    "\n",
    "# or\n",
    "image = soup.find(class_='image-container')\n",
    "image"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "af67bfbe-b21d-4883-8969-f4d9569e88d8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "If you have selected an element, you can access it's **attributes** using squared brackets:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "0a451165-42b7-4594-8c52-e82650b74084",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#image['class']\n",
    "\n",
    "#or\n",
    "image.attrs"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "70991d4d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Or you can check if a soup-object has certain attributes using: ``.has_attr('class')``"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "0903e06f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "image.has_attr('class')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "9ceed47c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "image"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3344b4a0-a3d3-447c-957c-5aa046dc0e93",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Elements of lower hierarchy levels**\n",
    "\n",
    "In the above example soup, the soup object is the tree and its children are the ``div`` blocks. If you want to access a tag within one of these div-blocks, you can do so like this:\n",
    "\n",
    "First, you select the section (using the `div`-tag) with the attribute `class` = 'image-container' and within it the tag `img`(the image) and it's attribute `src` "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "dc5efd59-e213-49ab-9a5b-f5b733b4d5a8",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "soup.find('div', class_='image-container').img['src']"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "34c5003c-7cd4-4f5d-8060-bc46f900bb48",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "You can also immediately search for the `img`- tag instead of first selecting the 'image-container' section anf then the `img`:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "a3430ef8-9ca3-4687-bd34-d6ef2d60e108",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "soup.find('img')['src']"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e45c7021-a684-479e-9a44-003f55b392ab",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Usually, there are many elements matching a certain search condition. To get all of them, you use:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "53fe0023-1063-4d7a-9577-78b45e9b9f54",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "divs = soup.find_all('div')\n",
    "divs"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "616fce70-7558-4196-ab2b-fce7092237be",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "This returns a list of all matched elements. If you now want to get the content (for example link or text) from one of the matches elements, you first have to index the list:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "29dca17b-31ca-48f3-b333-98a26a24f08b",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "div = divs[0]\n",
    "div.text#.strip()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "dd65c47f-2e94-43a3-8531-d9533ff7312d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "If you want to find all sections, where the class is of type 'image-container'/'link-container'/'text-container', you would search for all `div`-tags, where after splitting the `class` -attribute at the '-' sign, the element at position 1 (second part) equals the word 'container':"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "0ea166dc-fc59-46b4-847a-0111d5feef6d",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "soup.findAll('div', class_ = lambda x: x.split('-')[1] == \"container\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "982ca102",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<h4> 5.4 HTML tables using pandas</h4>\n",
    "\n",
    "Pandas [read_html()](https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/reference/api/pandas.read_html.html) is one of the easiest ways to scrape web data. The method takes either an HTML String or a URL as input. \n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "423d7690",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
